the democratic republic of congo is home  to the un's largest peacekeeping mission  yet a violent insurgency in the country  shows no signs of abating for nearly  three decades eastern congo has been  marred  with insecurity and frequent outbreaks  of violence caused by armed groups  last year more than 2 000 civilians were  killed in attacks  by these rebels now there are said to be  over 100 armed groups in the region and  experts say the deadliest among them  is the islamist group adf short for  allied democratic forces now the un's  monusco  mission which has been on the ground  since 2010 has deployed over 17  000 peacekeeping troops in the region  but as our dw team found the mission  has not brought peace or security to  people there  these are soldiers from south africa and  their task is to secure this region  which is considered a red zone because  of repeated attacks by the islamist  group adf  so we're going to follow them for one  day  it feels a bit odd to move in an armored  vehicle like this  while people outside seem to live a  normal everyday life  but the impression is deceptive this  area is dangerous  attacks happen almost on a daily basis  the islamist militia  adf has carried out repeated massacres  killing more than 500 people in the  second half of 2020.  we are on our way to the village of  maimoya very close to that village six  kilometers away there was an adf attack  two months ago  so this area where we're heading to is  considered as being secured but they can  always have an attack  with the un troops we visit a clinic in  maimoya  that has been targeted by adf in the  past  is the director here he's witnessed  gruesome attacks  the militia slaughters people and when  you see the animosity they have to kill  these people  how they cut off the heads you find  their brains scattered all over  they hack off feet that leaves you with  fear and traumatizes your heart  in one incident the islamists tried to  kidnap four  of his nurses  they found them in the waiting room and  took them hostage once people realized  it was adf they were very afraid but  then the militias said they won't kill  them they said  it is not a day to kill  because of the constant threat the  clinic is only open for a few hours in  the morning  women and children struggle to get the  medical care they need  they struggle with food they cannot go  and plow they cannot go and farm  they have to stay at home they cannot  move the children cannot go to school  a woman cannot come to the clinic as  well the villagers have experienced a  lot  but many are reluctant to speak to us  according to the intelligence officer  here at monusco  some villages are related to adf members  and feed them with information about  activities of the un troops and the  congolese army  the local population is in a tight  corner some of their sons and daughters  have joined  adf and often provide them with food but  in return the villagers have to  cooperate  and provide the militia group with  information or risk getting killed  sometimes we are happy and sometimes we  are afraid  because they can come today or tomorrow  they have now become our everyday people  just when we were about to leave a man  wants to speak to us out of fear he  doesn't want to be named  the government should help or the  international community should help our  government to deal with this phenomenon  that has made a lot of people suffer adf  should be finished  finished finished  his frustration is palpable and sums up  what many people here told us off-camera  their feeling is they are being brutally  massacred while the outside world  needs to act urgently  and we spoke to david zuma of the un  group of experts on the democratic  republic  of congo and also a senior researcher at  the institute of security studies in  pretoria we asked him if  the u.n peacekeeping mission in congo  had failed  look monisco is a response to a complex  problem  they have to deal with big territory and  they have to face 150  armed groups scattered around the  country  some of them are sponsored by  politicians who use them  to be to secure their position in  kinshasa the relation between monusco  and the government  previous administration under kabila has  also not been good  to facilitate the activities of the  institutions and you know  municipal work in the government near  the country where government is capable  to accompany what they have to do in  terms of peacekeeping this has been  lacking in the dlc for the past 10 years  but you really need to know exactly what  adf is you know there have been many  components  within what is called the allied  democracy force in in benin  a certain time they appear as ugandan  rebel group  who are trying to return to uganda to  challenge your muslim regime  at another time particularly since 2016  they have turned their guns against the  congolese  citizens now we are also hearing that  there might be some connection between  them and islamic state  in the region so you have all those in  one item and then you need to make a  difference  between the various components of that  institution to be able to engage in in  dialogue  the drc and international partners are  trying to win  other rebels you know in north korea in  south carolina province  through negotiation but they don't have  the logistical support they don't have a  financial support and there is no clear  plan  in terms of a ddr in place to absorb  those who might want to to leave the arm  group  to join the civilian civilian that they  have been experiences before  but those experiences have failed and  have not really produced a kind of  response responses that one could have  expected  for states to move to instability to  submitted peace i think the new  political dispensation and that way i  mean  in kinshasa is to see how this provides  an opportunity  for the drc government and various  actors to initiate  key reforms that will help the country  to move forward  the one front is simply to review the  electoral process to allow citizens to  express their will  on who they want to to lead the country  legitimacy the second one is also to  undertake  institutional reforms that would have a  dlc to respond to the basic need of  the congolese citizen and what is  happening now in terms of a sacred union  or union with political forces could  provide a momentum for this  to help the effort of international  community to take their seat forward  but it's not going to be something that  is going to happen overnight they're  going to take a bit of time  but between now and 2023 i think if the  jersey government  work with the momentum or current  momentum that can send a strong message  that the drc is capable of overcoming  some of the key challenges that we just  highlight
 

